On Thu, Sep 4, 2014 at 7:22 AM, selpa'i <seladwa@gmx.de> wrote:

> la'o me. Martin Bays .me cusku
>
>  (iii) Have it pick out some referents such that they satisfy the
>> restrictives:
>>      ko'a poi broda -> zo'e noi me ko'a gi'e broda
>>
>
> This is pretty much my take on it.


Me too. Others have argued though that the poi-clause is (at least
sometimes) not a restriction on the pre-established referents of ko'a, but
rather a way to identify what the referents of ko'a are:
https://groups.google.com/forum/#!topic/lojban/UUzglNqQg70

<<
(1) {ti poi toldi}: At least in the way my mind works, when I point at
something, I point at a specific thing; I just need to give a clue to the
listener about what I am exactly pointing at. It is not like I am pointing
at a bunch of things and selecting a butterfly out of them.
>>

To me "ti poi toldi" for "this butterfly/these butterflies" always sounded
off, because it starts with these things and then restricts them to those
that are butterflies, instead of starting with butterflies and then
restricting them to those that are near-by. That's why "lo vi toldi" makes
more sense to me.

mu'o mi'e xorxes

